mber of distinct ordered quadruples might be fewer.\n\nSo we should count ordered quadruples by considering each valid (u,v) and valid assignment of M,N, and then for each, we have two candidate orders for a,b and two for c,d, giving 4 ordered quadruples. But we must also check that the quadratics yield integer roots, i.e., discriminants are perfect squares. We already did that.\n\nLet\'s continue systematically.\n\nWe have uv=14, and we found that the factorization (u,v)=(-1,-14) with M=-2,N=-13 gives valid integer roots: a,b from x^2 - ux + X with u=-1, X=-30 gives roots 5 and -6; c,d from x^2 - vx + Y with v=-14, Y=-15 gives roots 1 and -15. So the numbers are {5, -6, 1, -15}. Check: Are these all distinct? Yes. So we get 4 ordered quadruples: (5,-6,1,-15), (-6,5,1,-15), (5,-6,-15,1), (-6,5,-15,1). Also the swapped assignment M=-13,N=-2 for (u,v)=(-14,-1) gives valid roots: a,b from u=-14, X=-15 gives roots 1 and -15; c,d from v=-1, Y=-30 gives roots 5 and -6. That yields quadruples: (1,-15,5,-6), (-15,1,5,-6), (1,-15,-6,5), (-15,1,-6,5). But these are actually the same set of numbers as before, but in different order. However, note: In the first case, we had (a,b)=(5,-6) and (c,d)=(1,-15); in the second, (a,b)=(1,-15) and (c,d)=(5,-6). So they are different ordered quadruples if we consider the assignment of which pair is (a,b) and which is (c,d). But are they distinct? Yes, because in the first, the first two numbers are 5 and -6; in the second, the first two are 1 and -15. So they are distinct ordered quadruples. So both combinations yield 4 ordered quadruples each. So for uv=14, we have two valid (u,v) pairs: (-1,-14) and (-14,-1), each with one valid assignment of M,N (either the first or the second order of M,N) gives the same set of four numbers but with different labeling of the pairs. However, if we consider the two assignments separately, they actually yield the same set of numbers, but with different pairing of which sum is u and which is v. But if we 