,-1): X = -6+2*(-1)= -8, Y = -10+2*(-15)= -10-30= -40. D1 = 225 - 4*(-8)=225+32=257, not square. D2 = 1 - 4*(-40)=1+160=161, not square.\n\n(u,v)=(3,-5): uv=3*(-5)=-15, not 15. So we need uv=15, so u and v must both be positive or both negative. So consider positive pairs: (1,15), (3,5), (5,3), (15,1). Also negative pairs (as above).\n\nWe already did (-1,-15) and (-15,-1). Try (u,v)=(3,5): X = -6+2*5=4, Y = -10+2*3=-4. D1 = 9 - 16= -7, negative. Invalid.\n\n(u,v)=(5,3): X = -6+2*3=0, Y = -10+2*5=0. Both X and Y zero, so ab=0 and cd=0, not allowed.\n\n(u,v)=(1,15): X = -6+30=24, Y = -10+2= -8. D1 = 1 - 96= -95, negative. Invalid.\n\nSo no valid for uv=15.\n\nCase uv=20.\n\nM,N = -5, -16.\n\nFactor pairs with uv=20: (1,20), (2,10), (4,5), (5,4), (10,2), (20,1) and negatives.\n\nTry negative pairs:\n\n(u,v)=(-1,-20): X = -5+2*(-20)= -5-40= -45, Y = -16+2*(-1)= -16-2= -18. D1 = 1 - 4*(-45)=1+180=181, not square. D2 = 400 - 4*(-18)=400+72=472, not square.\n\n(u,v)=(-2,-10): X = -5+2*(-10)= -5-20= -25, Y = -16+2*(-2)= -16-4= -20. D1 = 4 - 4*(-25)=4+100=104, not square. D2 = 100 - 4*(-20)=100+80=180, not square.\n\n(u,v)=(-4,-5): X = -5+2*(-5)= -5-10= -15, Y = -16+2*(-4)= -16-8= -24. D1 = 16 - 4*(-15)=16+60=76, not square. D2 = 25 - 4*(-24)=25+96=121=11^2. So D2 is square, D1 not. So invalid.\n\n(u,v)=(-5,-4): X = -5+2*(-4)= -5-8= -13, Y = -16+2*(-5)= -16-10= -26. D1 = 25 - 4*(-13)=25+52=77, not square. D2 = 16 - 4*(-26)=16+104=120, not square.\n\nTry positive pairs:\n\n(u,v)=(1,20): X = -5+40=35, Y = -16+2= -14. D1 = 1 - 140= -139, negative.\n\n(u,v)=(2,10): X = -5+20=15, Y = -16+4= -12. D1 = 4 - 60= -56, negative.\n\n(u,v)=(4,5): X = -5+10=5, Y = -16+8= -8. D1 = 16 - 20= -4, negative.\n\n(u,v)=(5,4): X = -5+8=3, Y = -16+10= -6. D1 = 25 - 12=13, not square. D2 = 16 - 4*(-6)=16+24=40, not square.\n\n(u,v)=(10,2): X = -5+4= -1, Y = -16+20=4. D1 = 100 - 4*(-1)=100+4=104, not square. D2 = 4 - 16= -12, negative.\n\n(u,v)=(20,1): X = -5+2= -3, Y = -16+40=24. D1 = 400 - 4*(-3)=400+12=412, not square. D2 = 1 - 96= -95, negative.\n\nSo no valid for uv=20.\n\nCase uv=-1.\n\nM,N = 2, -2.\n\nFactor pairs with uv=-1: (1,-1), (-1,1). Also (other factor pairs? Since uv=-1, the only integer factor pairs are (1,-1) and (-1,1). Also (u,v) = (1,-1) and (-1,1). Possibly also (u,v) = (c,d)? But uv=-1, so u=1, v=-1 or u=-1, v=1.\n\nTry (u,v)=(1,-1): X = M+2v = using M=2, N=-2? We need to decide which assignment. Let\'s first try M=2, N=-2. Then X = 2+2*(-1)=0, Y = -2+2*1=0. So X=0, Y=0, not allowed (ab=0, cd=0). Invalid.\n\nTry swapped: M=-2, N=2. Then X = -2+2*(-1)= -4, Y = 2+2*1=4. D1 = u^2 - 4X = 1 - 4*(-4)=1+16=17, not square. D2 = v^2 - 4Y = 1 - 4*4=1-16=-15, negative. Invalid.\n\nSo uv=-1 yields no valid.\n\nCase uv=-6.\n\nM,N = 8, -3.\n\nFactor pairs with uv=-6: (1,-6), (2,-3), (3,-2), (6,-1), (-1,6), (-2,3), (-3,2), (-6,1).\n\nTry (u,v)=(-1,6): X = 8+2*6=8+12=20, Y = -3+2*(-1)= -3-2= -5. D1 = 1 - 4*20=1-80=-79, negative. Invalid.\n\n(u,v)=(-2,3): X = 8+2*3=8+6=14, Y = -3+2*(-2)= -3-4= -7. D1 = 4 - 4*14=4-56=-52, negative.\n\n(u,v)=(-3,2): X = 8+2*2=8+4=12, Y = -3+2*(-3)= -3-6= -9. D1 = 9 - 4*12=9-48=-39, negative.\n\n(u,v)=(-6,1): X = 8+2*1=8+2=10, Y = -3+2*(-6)= -3-12= -15. D1 = 36 - 4*10=36-40=-4, negative? -4 is negative, so not square (and also negative discriminant, so no real roots, but integer roots require discriminant ≥0 and perfect square; -4 is not a perfect square and negative).\n\nTry swapped assignments: For each pair, we can also try M=-3, N=8.\n\nFor (u,v)=(-1,6) with M=-3,N=8: X = -3+2*6= -3+12=9, Y = 8+2*(-1)=8-2=6. D1 = 1 - 36= -35, negative. Invalid.\n\n(u,v)=(-2,3) with M=-3,N=8: X = -3+2*3= -3+6=3, Y = 8+2*(-2)=8-4=4. D1 = 4 - 12= -8, negative.\n\n(u,v)=(-3,2) with M=-3,N=8: X = -3+2*2= -3+4=1, Y = 8+2*(-3)=8-6=2. D1 = 9 - 4=5, not square. D2 = 4 - 8= -4, negative.\n\n(u,v)=(-6,1) with M=-3,N=8: X = -3+2*1= -3+2= -1, Y = 8+2*(-6)=8-12= -4. D1 = 36 - 4*(-1)=36+4=40, not square. D2 = 1 - 4*(-4)=1+16=17, not square.\n\nSo no valid for uv=-6.\n\nSo only uv=14 yielded valid solutions, with unordered set {5, -6, 1, -15}.\n\nBut wait, we found two valid assignments: one from (u,v)=(-1,-14) with M=-2,N=-13, and one from (u,v)=(-14,-1) with M=-13,N=-2. Both gave the same unordered set {5,-6,1,-15}. So that is one unordered set.\n\nAre there any other unordered sets? Possibly from uv=14 with other factorizations? We only checked (u,v)=(-1,-14) and (u,v)=(-14,-1). But there are also factorizations like (u,v)=(2,-7)? That gives uv=-14, not 14. For uv=14, we need both u and v positive or both negative. So only (1,14), (2,7), (14,1) and negatives. We checked those and only (-1,-14) and (-14,-1) worked with the right M,N assignment. But we also need to check if there are other M,N possibilities? For uv=14, M and N are fixed at (-2,-13) regardless of u,v. We considered both orders. So that covers all.\n\nThus we have one unordered set: {5, -6, 1, -15}. But is that the only one? Let\'s double-check for uv=14 with (u,v)=(-1,-14) and M=-2,N=-13 we got roots 5,-6 and 1,-15. Are these all distinct? Yes.\n\nBut maybe there are other solutions where uv=14 but with different M,N? No, M,N are determined uniquely by uv.\n\nSo only one unordered set.\n\nNow we need to count the number of ordered quadruples from this set. The numbers are 5, -6, 1, -15. Are they all distinct? Yes, they are four distinct integers. So how many ordered quadruples are there? There are 4! = 24 permutations. But do all permutations satisfy the equations? The equations are symmetric, so if (5,-6,1,-15) is a solution, then any permutation of these four numbers is also a solution. So there should be 24 ordered quadruples.\n\nBut wait, we need to check if the quadruple (5,-6,1,-15) indeed satisfies both equations. We checked the original system? We derived it from our method, but we should verify.\n\nCheck (5,-6,1,-15):\nEquation (1): 1/5 + 1/(-6) + 1/1 + 1/(-15) = 1/5 - 1/6 + 1 - 1/15 = common denominator 30: (6/30 - 5/30 + 30/30 - 2/30) = (6-5+30-2)/30 = 29/30 = 0.966..., not 1/2. That\'s not 1/2. Did we make a mistake? Let\'s compute carefully:\n\n1/5 = 0.2, 1/(-6) ≈ -0.1667, 1/1=1, 1/(-15) ≈ -0.0667. Sum = 0.2 - 0.1667 + 1 - 0.0667 = 1.0666? Actually: 0.2 - 0.1666667 = 0.0333333, plus 1 = 1.0333333, minus 0.0666667 = 0.9666666. So 29/30 = 0.9667, not 0.5. So it does not satisfy equation (1). Something is wrong.\n\nMaybe our derivation had an error? We used Vieta and the condition from equation (1) as R = P/2. But let\'s check for (5,-6,1,-15): P = abcd = 5*(-6)*1*(-15) = 5*(-6)*1*(-15) = 5*(-6) = -30, times 1 = -30, times -15 = 450. So P=450. R = abc+abd+acd+bcd. Compute: abc = 5*(-6)*1 = -30; abd = 5*(-6)*(-15)= 5*(-6)*(-15)=500; acd = 5*1*(-15)= -75; bcd = (-6)*1*(-15)= 90. So R = -30+500-75+90=485. Then R/P = 485/450 = 97/90 ≈ 1.0778, not 1/2. So indeed it does not satisfy equation (1). But our derivation gave (a-1)(b-1)(c-1)(d-1) = S + P/2. For this set, S = 5-6+1-15 = -15, P=450, P/2=225, so S+P/2 = 210, and (a-1)(b-1)(c-1)(d-1) = (4)*(-7)*(0)*(-16)=0? Actually: (5-1)=4, (-6-1)=-7, (1-1)=0, (-15-1)=-16, product = 0. So not equal. So that quadruple does not satisfy the derived conditions? But we thought it did from our M,N condition. So there is a discrepancy.\n\nMaybe I made an error in computing M,N? For uv=14, we had M,N = -2, -13. For (u,v)=(-1,-14), X = -2+2v = -2+2*(-14) = -2-28= -30, Y = -13+2u = -13+2*(-1)= -13-2= -15. Then D1 = u^2 - 4X = 1 -4*(-30)=1+120=121=11^2, so a,b = (u ± √D1)/2 = (-1 ± 11)/2 = (10/2, -12/2) = (5, -6). D2 = v^2 - 4Y = 196 -4*(-15)=196+60=256=16^2, so c,d = (v ± √D2)/2 = (-14 ± 16)/2 = (2/2, -30/2) = (1, -15). So that gave (5,-6,1,-15). But then we need to check if it satisfies the original equations. It doesn\'t satisfy (1). So maybe our conditions are not sufficient? Or maybe I made a mistake in the derivation?\n\nLet\'s derive again carefully.\n\nWe had:\nEquation (2): 2(a+b+c+d) = ab+cd+(a+b)(c+d)+1.\nWe defined u=a+b, v=c+d, X=ab, Y=cd.\nThen (a+b)(c+d)=uv. So equation (2) becomes: 2(u+v)= X+Y+uv+1 => X+Y = 2(u+v)-uv-1.\nEquation (1): 1/a+1/b+1/c+1/d = 1/2 => (a+b)/(ab) + (c+d)/(cd) = 1/2 => u/X + v/Y = 1/2 => 2uY + 2vX = XY.\nSo we have:\nX+Y = 2(u+v) - uv - 1.\n2uY + 2vX = XY.\nWe then derived (X-2v)(Y-2u) = 4uv.\nFrom that, we set M=X-2v, N=Y-2u, then M+N = X+Y-2u-2v = (2(u+v)-uv-1) -2(u+v) = -uv-1.\nAnd MN = (X-2v)(Y-2u) = 4uv.\nSo M and N satisfy: M+N = -uv-1, MN=4uv.\nSo that is correct.\n\nThen we solved for uv, getting uv ∈ {14,15,20,-1,-6,0} and discards 0. Then for each uv, we found M,N.\n\nFor uv=14, we had M,N = -2, -13.\n\nThen we found a factorization (u,v)=(-1,-14) that gave X = M+2v = -2+2*(-14)= -30, Y = N+2u = -13+2*(-1)= -15. Then we computed discriminants and got integer roots. So that seemed to satisfy the conditions derived from equation (2) and the transformation from equation (1)? But we didn\'t check equation (1) explicitly. Actually, from M+N and MN we derived conditions that must hold if X and Y are to satisfy the two equations. But we also used the quadratic discriminants to ensure that a,b and c,d are integers. So all conditions are encoded in the M,N and the discriminant condition. So if we got integer a,b and c,d from that, they should satisfy both equations? Let\'s check directly if they satisfy equation (2).\n\nCheck (5,-6,1,-15):\nCompute ab = 5*(-6)= -30, cd = 1*(-15)= -15, (a+b)= -1, (c+d)= -14, so (a+b)(c+d)= (-1)*(-14)=14.\nRight side of equation (2): ab+cd+(a+b)(c+d)+1 = -30 + (-15) + 14 + 1 = -30-15+14+1 = -30. Left side: 2(a+b+c+d)=2(-1 -15)=2*(-16)= -32. So -30 ≠ -32. So it does not satisfy equation (2). So there is an error: For (5,-6,1,-15), our computed X and Y are ab=-30, cd=-15, (a+b)(c+d)=14, so right side = -30-15+14+1=-30. Left side: a+b+c+d = -1-15=-16, times 2 = -32. So not equal. So why did our derivation yield this? Maybe because we used M,N to compute X and Y, but we also assumed that a,b are roots of x^2 - ux + X = 0. For u=-1, X=-30, the quadratic is x^2 + x -30=0, roots: ( -1 ± √(1+120) )/2 = (-1 ± 11)/2, so roots are 5 and -6. That\'s correct. For v=-14, Y=-15, quadratic: x^2 +14x -15=0? Actually v=-14, so quadratic is x^2 - vx + Y = x^2 +14x -15=0? Wait: x^2 - vx + Y, with v=-14, so -v = +14, so it\'s x^2 +14x -15=0. Discriminant: 196+60=256, roots: (-14 ± 16)/2 = 1 and -15. That\'s correct. So we have a,b and c,d as these numbers. But then equation (2) should hold. Let\'s recompute equation (2) carefully:\n\nEquation (2): 2(a+b+c+d) = ab+cd+(a+b)(c+d)+1.\nFor our quadruple: a=5, b=-6, c=1, d=-15.\na+b = -1, c+d = -14.\nab = 5*(-6)= -30.\ncd = 1*(-15)= -15.\n(a+b)(c+d)= (-1)*(-14)=14.\nRight side: -30 + (-15) + 14 + 1 = -30-15+14+1 = -30.\nLeft side: 2(5-6+1-15)= 2*(-15)= -30.\nWait, left side is 2*(a+b+c+d) = 2*(-1 + -14) = 2*(-15) = -30. So left side is also -30. They are equal! I computed earlier left side as 2*(-1 -15) but that\'s wrong: a+b+c+d = (-1) + (-14) = -15, not -16. I mistakenly added the roots separately: a+b is -1, c+d is -14, sum is -15. So left side = -30. Right side = -30. So it satisfies equation (2)! Good.\n\nNow check equation (1): 1/5 + 1/(-6) + 1/1 + 1/(-15) = 1/5 - 1/6 + 1 - 1/15 = let\'s compute exactly: common denominator 30: 6/30 - 5/30 + 30/30 - 2/30 = (6-5+30-2)/30 = 29/30. That is not 1/2 = 15/30. So it fails equation (1). But we derived that equation (1) implies R = P/2. For this quadruple, P = 5*(-6)*1*(-15)=450, P/2=225, R = abc+abd+acd+bcd = 5*(-6)*1= -30, abd=5*(-6)*(-15)=450, acd=5*1*(-15)= -75, bcd=(-6)*1*(-15)=90, sum = -30+450-75+90=485. So R=485, not equal to 225. So equation (1) is not satisfied. But from our derivation, we used equation (1) to get u/X+v/Y=1/2, which gave 2uY+2vX=XY. That condition was satisfied for our numbers? Let\'s check: 2uY+2vX = 2*(-6)*(-15)+2*5*(-1) = 2*90 + 2*(-5)=180-10=170; XY = (-30)*(-15)=450. So 170 ≠ 450. So the condition 2uY+2vX=XY is not satisfied. But we derived it from equation (1): u/X + v/Y = 1/2 => multiply by 2XY: 2uY+2vX=XY. So it should