cond from the third? Or we can solve linearly. There are 4 variables and 3 equations. The general solution will be a linear combination of some independent vectors.\n\nWe can compute the rank of the coefficient matrix. The matrix is:\n[1 1 1 1]\n[1 2 3 4]\n[1 4 2 2]\n(I am using indices 1,2,3,4). Compute determinant of this 3x4 matrix. We can do row operations. Subtract row1 from row2 and row3:\nRow2 - Row1: (0, 1, 2, 3)\nRow3 - Row1: (0, 3, 1, 1)\nNow we have:\n[1,1,1,1]\n[0,1,2,3]\n[0,3,1,1]\nNow subtract 3*row2 from row3: row3 - 3*row2 gives (0,0, -5, -8) mod 7 = (0,0,2,6) mod 7 (since -5 ≡ 2, -8 ≡ 6 mod 7). So row3 becomes (0,0,2,6). So the matrix is:\n[1,1,1,1]\n[0,1,2,3]\n[0,0,2,6]\nThis is clearly of rank 3 (since the first three columns are independent). So the equations are independent. So the solution space is 1-dimensional. Let (y1,...,y4) be a solution. We can express y2 and y4 in terms of y1 and y3. From row2 and row3:\ny1+2y2+3y3+4y4=0 => 2y2+3y3+4y4 = -y1.\ny1+4y2+2y3+2y4=0 => 4y2+2y3+2y4 = -y1.\nWe can solve these two linear equations for y2 and y4 in terms of y1 and y3. Let’s do that. Multiply the first equation by 2: 4y2+6y3+8y4 = -2y1. Subtract the second equation multiplied by? Alternatively, solve simultaneously.\nLet’s denote:\nA: 2y2 + 3y3 + 4y4 = -y1\nB: 4y2 + 2y3 + 2y4 = -y1\nMultiply B by 2: 8y2 + 4y3 + 4y4 = -2y1.\nSubtract A from this: (8y2 - 2y2) + (4y3 - 3y3) + (4y4 - 4y4) = -2y1 + y1 => 6y2 + y3 = -y1.\nSo 6y2 + y3 = -y1 => 6y2 = -y1 - y3 => y2 = (-y1 - y3)/6 mod 7. Since 6 is invertible mod 7 (6*6=36≡1 mod 7), inverse of 6 is 6? Actually, 6*6=36≡1, so 6 is its own inverse. So y2 = 6*(-y1 - y3) = -6y1 -6y3 mod 7.\nNow substitute back into one equation to find y4. From B: 4y2+2y3+2y4 = -y1 => 2y4 = -y1 - 4y2 - 2y3 => y4 = (-y1 - 4y2 - 2y3)/2 mod 7. Inverse of 2 mod 7 is 4 (since 2*4=8≡1). So y4 = 4*(-y1 - 4y2 - 2y3) = -4y1 -16y2 -8y3 mod 7 = -4y1 -2y2 -1y3 mod 7 (since 16≡2, 8≡1). Substitute y2: y4 = -4y1 -2(-6y1 -6y3) - y3 = -4y1 +12y1