y 4 elements of order 5. But the problem asks "Determine whether there are exactly 4 elements of order 5..." So we need to answer: Yes, there are exactly 4. But maybe we should double-check: Could there be more? In a cyclic group of order 1210, the number of elements of order exactly 5 is φ(5)=4, so that\'s correct. However, we should also consider if the group is indeed cyclic. Actually, the group $(\\mathbb{Z}/p^n\\mathbb{Z})^*$ is not necessarily cyclic if p=2. But for odd primes, it is cyclic. That is a standard result. So we can confidently say that there are exactly 4.\n\nBut wait: Could there be elements of order 5 that are not generators of the unique subgroup of order 5? Possibly in non-cyclic groups, there could be more? But here it\'s cyclic, so no.\n\nLet\'s confirm: The order of the group is 1210. Compute φ(5)=4. So the answer should be yes.\n\nHowever, maybe the problem expects an answer: "Yes" or "No". Since the question says "Determine whether there are exactly 4 elements of order 5...", we need to answer with a determination: either "there are exactly 4" or "there are not exactly 4". Given our reasoning, we answer: Yes.\n\nBut let\'s be thorough: Could there be elements of order 5 that are not in the cyclic group of order 5? In a cyclic group, every element of a given order corresponds to an element in the unique subgroup of that order. Since the subgroup of order 5 exists (because 5 divides 1210), it has φ(5)=4 generators. So indeed, there are exactly 4.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\boxed{Yes}. But the instruction says "put your final answer within \\boxed{}". Typically, we put the answer statement inside the box. Possibly: \\boxed{\\text{Yes}} or \\boxed{\\text{There are exactly 4 elements of order 5}}? Maybe just "Yes". Alternatively, some math problems expect a simple "Yes" or "No". I\'ll put \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\n\nBut let\'s consider if there is any nuance: Sometimes the group $(\\mathbb{Z}/1331\\mathbb{Z})^*$ is cyclic only for p odd? Yes. Also, note that 1331 = 11^3, so it\'s a prime power. So it\'s cyclic.\n\nMaybe we should also consider if there are elements of order 5 that are not primitive roots of the subgroup? Actually, in a cyclic group of order n, the number of elements of order d is φ(d) if d divides n. So here d=5 divides 1210, so yes.\n\nThus, the answer is affirmative.\n\nI\'ll answer: \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\n</think>\n\nThe group of units modulo 1331 is \\((\\mathbb{Z}/1331\\mathbb{Z})^*\\), which is cyclic because 1331 is an odd prime power (\\(11^3\\)). Its order is \\(\\varphi(1331) = 1210\\). In a cyclic group of order 1210, the number of elements of order 5 is given by Euler\'s totient function \\(\\varphi(5) = 4\\), since 5 divides 1210. Therefore, there are exactly 4 elements of order 5.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}<|im_end|>'], label: Yes, reward: 1
