actors.\n\nWe need a discount factor per day. Since interest is credited monthly with monthly rate 0.005 (interpreted as 0.5% per month), and each month has 30 days, the interest earned per day if the balance is constant is 0.005/30 = 0.0001667 per day, or 1.667% per day. But if the balance changes, it\'s more complicated.\n\nBut for constant balances, we can approximate by using a daily discount factor based on the monthly rate. Because for a cash flow that occurs at the end of a month, we can use an effective monthly discount factor of 1/(1+0.005) = 0.995, but careful: If interest is credited at the end, then a cashflow occurring at the end of the month is discounted based on one less month. But since we are discounting backward, we can compute the discount factors per month and then per day.\n\nSimplify: Use monthly periods. The cash flows occur at days: 30 and 60. So we have two months: first month (days 0-30), second month (days 30-60). For the first month, interest is credited if there is a balance at the end of the month. For debit, if you have no balance at the end of month 0 (since you paid), then no interest. So interest cashflow at day 60: 30 rubles added at the end of month 1. That cashflow is already earned from the balance in month 1. So to find its value at day 0, we can discount it by the interest rate that would have applied to it if it had been there from day 0. Since it was solely in month 1, it will earn interest from day 30. So its PV at day 0 is: 30 * discount factor for 60 days from day 60 to day 0. We\'ll need a daily discount factor.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute the total value of the account at day 0 by considering the "gains" from the credit card, and directly compare the cashflows at day 0.\n\nMaybe we can think in terms of net benefit at a common point: The debit option gives a benefit of cashback and interest, while credit option gives cashback and requires repayment at time N. We want to find N such that paying credit is better.\n\nLet’s compute the NPV for debit option explicitly, assuming a daily discount rate derived from the 6% annual rate. Since each month has 30 days, and interest is credited at the end of each month, the effective 360-day annual rate? Actually, the monthly rate is 0.5% if we take 0.06/12 = 0.005, but as argued, that yields 1.8% per year if compounded. But maybe they mean that the nominal annual rate is 6%, so the monthly rate is 0.5%, and it is not compounded, so the annual effective rate is still 6%? That would require that the interest is calculated as: For each month, interest = (balance * 0.005) * ? Actually, if the annual rate is 6%, then the interest earned over the year on a constant balance B is 0.06B. If the months have 30 days, then the interest for a month is 0.06*30? That doesn\'t work.\n\nI think the most sensible interpretation is: The annual interest rate is 6%, meaning that if you leave money in the account for a full year, you earn 6% on it. So effectively, the daily interest rate (if compounded daily) would be approximately 0.06/365, but they assume 30 days per month for simplicity in computing average balances. So for average balance over a 30-day month, the interest for that month is 6% * (30/365)? That seems messy.\n\nGiven the problem statement, they likely want us to treat the monthly interest rate as 0.5% (since 6%/12 = 0.5%), and then treat the interest as simple for each month, just as in the formula: Interest for a month = average balance * 0.5% * 30? That gives 1.5% per month, not 0.5%. So that\'s inconsistent.\n\nWait: Maybe they mean: annual interest rate 6%, so the monthly interest rate is 0.5%, and interest is not compounded, so the interest for a month is simply: balance * 0.5% (per month) over 30 days? That would be balance * 0.005, not multiplied by 30. That seems like a daily rate: 0.5% per month is a monthly rate, but if you multiply by days, you get annual. So perhaps the intended calculation is: If you have a balance of B at the beginning of a month and it remains constant for 30 days, then at the end of the month, you get interest equal to B * 0.005 * 30 = B * 0.15. That means the monthly interest rate is 0.15, not 0.005. So the monthly interest rate is 15%. That would mean the annual interest rate is 15% * 12 = 180%, which is not 6%. So that\'s clearly wrong.\n\nGiven the confusion, I\'ll look for similar problems. Often in such problems, the annual interest rate is used as a monthly rate by dividing by 12, and then interest for a month is computed as: interest = balance * (annual rate/12) * number of days. So with annual rate 6%, monthly rate = 0.5%, and interest for a month = balance * 0.005 * 30. That gives a monthly interest factor of 0.15. That is a huge factor. But then the annual factor would be 12*0.15 = 1.8, i.e., 180% interest. So that\'s unrealistic. Alternatively, maybe they mean that the interest is credited at the end of each month as a simple interest on the average balance, and the annual rate is 6% effective, so the monthly interest rate is (1 - (balance/12))^12? Actually, no.\n\nGiven the typical banking, when they say annual interest rate, it\'s usually an effective annual rate. So if it\'s 6% effective annual, then the monthly compounding factor is (1+0.06)^(1/12) ≈ 1.005, so monthly rate ≈ 0.5%. So the monthly rate is approximately 0.5%. And if it\'s not compounded, then for a balance constant over 30 days, the interest is balance * 0.005 * 30? That would be 15% per month. That is not 6%. So to get 6% annual, we need a monthly rate of 0.5% if compounded monthly, but if not compounded, the interest would be less. Actually, if it\'s simple interest for the whole year: interest = balance * 0.06, because over the year the annual rate is applied. So if the balance is constant, then the interest for the year is simply 0.06 * balance. That suggests that they treat the average monthly balance as the amount each month, and the interest is applied per month on that monthly amount. So if you have a constant balance B over the year, then each month has the same balance B, and the interest charged per month is 0.06 * B. That is simple interest for the year. But then they mention "credited at the end of each month" and "not compounded", so maybe the interest is charged monthly but not added to the balance to earn more interest. So it\'s simple interest per month: For each month, interest = 0.06 * (balance per month). But the balance per month is the average daily balance for that month, not the overall balance. If the balance is constant at B over the year, then the average daily balance per month is B (if constant). So interest per month = 0.06 * B. That would make the total annual interest = 0.06 * B * 12 = 0.72 * B, which is 72% of B. That is still not 6% per year. So that doesn\'t work.\n\nThe only consistent interpretation is that the 6% annual rate is the nominal annual rate, and the monthly interest rate is 0.5%, and since it\'s simple interest, the interest for a month is balance * 0.5% * 30 = 15% of balance. That yields 180% per year. So maybe the 6% is actually the effective rate after compounding? But they say not compounded.\n\nI\'ll check the phrasing: "the annual interest rate on the average monthly balance of funds on the debit card is 6% per year (Assume for simplicity that each month has 30 days, the interest is credited to the card at the end of each month, and the interest accrued on the balance is not compounded)." That suggests that the interest is calculated on the average monthly balance, and you get 6% per year on that average monthly balance. So if you have an average monthly balance of X rubles per month, you get 0.06X at the end of the year. That implies that the interest is not distributed monthly; it\'s a lump sum at the end of the year. But then they say it is credited at the end of each month. That is contradictory. Possibly they mean: interest is calculated monthly on the average monthly balance, but since it\'s not compounded, it\'s simply interest = average monthly balance * (6%/12) * 30? That still gives 1.8%. \n\nGiven the typical style of such problems, I think they expect us to use the monthly interest rate as 0.6% (since 6%/12 = 0.5%) and treat the interest as simple interest on the average balance for that month, multiplied by 0.5% of the average balance times 30 days? That would be weird. Alternatively, maybe they want us to ignore the 30 days in the interest calculation and just use monthly compounding? Often, when they say "each month has 30 days", they just mean that for interest calculations, we treat each month as 30 days, but the interest rate is annual, so we convert to a monthly rate by dividing by 12, and then we assume that interest is compounded monthly. But they explicitly say "not compounded". So it\'s not compounded.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps the intended approach is to compare the net present values of the cashbacks and the cost of borrowing without interest. The key point is that if you borrow the 20,000 through credit, you can earn interest on the balance on the debit card because you don\'t exhaust the funds immediately. On debit, you pay immediately, so the balance on the debit card is reduced, and you only get cashback after 1 month. On credit, you keep the 20,000 on a credit card that would normally accrue interest if you didn\'t repay within N days. So the advantage of credit is that it allows you to maintain the 20,000 on the debit card (or rather, relieves the depletion of funds on the debit card) so that you can earn interest on it. The disadvantage is that you receive less cashback (0.5% vs 1%) and you have to repay within N days.\n\nSo we need to find N such that the total interest earned on the debit card over the borrowing period (if you had the 20,000 on that card) exceeds the cashback difference. Plus the repayment has a cost of interest on the credit card if you don\'t repay exactly at N days? But wait: The problem says you repay within N days to avoid extra charges. So if you repay exactly at day N, you pay no interest on the credit card. So there is no interest cost on the credit card. So the only cost difference is the lower cashback on credit. But also, on credit, you have the flexibility to keep the funds available longer because you spent N days paying, but actually you still repaid at N days, so your funds are reduced at time N. However, from time 0 to time N, you have used the credit for the purchase, so your available funds are still reduced by 20,000 at time N? Actually, at time 0, you pay 20,000 via credit, so you have 20,000 not used yet (since you owe the bank). But that is money you don\'t have in your checking account. So effectively, from time 0 to time N, your available funds are less than on debit (where you paid immediately). However, on debit, you don\'t have that 20,000 anywhere because you paid it. On credit, you have a debt of 20,000 that you eventually pay. But that debt does not earn interest because you repay within N days. So interestingly, both options use up 20,000 from your checking account, but credit uses it as a loan that you pay back quickly. So the funding from your checking accounts is the same: you pay 20,000 at time 0 in both cases. The only difference is that on credit, you have the promise to pay back within N days, which avoids interest on the credit card. So the cashback is different. But also, the credit card might allow you to keep the 20,000 on the debit card longer? No: On debit, you withdraw 20,000 immediately for the purchase. On credit, you debit that 20,000 via credit card, so no money is removed from your checking account. So at time 0, on both options, your checking account balance is reduced by 20,000. However, from time 0 to time N, the money on the debit card is still available? Actually, on debit, after the purchase, your checking account has 20,000 less. On credit, after the purchase, your Checking account still has 20,000 (you owe the credit card), and you also have the funds on the credit card (which is a line of credit). So potentially, you could use the funds on the credit card? But that is just a loan, not a budget. So at time 0, the net available funds in your checking account are the same (20,000 cheaper). But the cashback on debit is received after 1 month, while on credit it is also received after 1 month. So the timing of cashbacks is the same. However, on credit, you have the option to keep the 20,000 on the credit card that might earn interest if you don\'t repay, but you repay within N days, so no interest. So the only difference is cashback.\n\nMaybe the intended comparison is between the benefit of earning interest on the debit card versus the benefit of lower cashback. But wait: If you pay with credit card and repay within N days, you don\'t incur interest on the credit card, but you also don\'t have the 20,000 available on the debit card? Actually, both options: On debit, you pay 20,000 immediately, so that money leaves your account. On credit, you take a loan of 20,000, so you still have that money but it\'s on a loan. The money on the loan is not available for investment/interest earning until you repay. So net, the funding of 20,000 comes from somewhere: on debit, you use your checking account; on credit, you also use your checking account because you didn\'t have the money at time 0. So the saving is that with credit, you can keep the 20,000 uncommitted for N days because you only repay at time N. So your checking account has less slack (i.e., more available funds) from time 0 to time N because the 20,000 hasn\'t been reduced. But on debit, the checking account is reduced immediately by 20,000, so you have less available funds from time 0 to time N. So that means you earn more interest on the debit card if you keep it longer because you don\'t have funds available to withdraw? Actually, you have less funds available, so you earn interest on a smaller balance? That\'s opposite. Typically, interest is earned on balances you hold. If you have less balance, you earn less interest. So on debit, you have only 20,000 (if you start with 20,000) but you use it immediately, so you earn interest on the 20,000 from time 0 to time N? Actually, on debit, you pay 20,000 at time 0, so you withdraw that 20,000, so your balance becomes below 20,000 if you started with more. But if you started with exactly 20,000, then after payment you have 0, so you don\'t hold any funds from time 0 to time N. So you earn no interest on the debit card initially. Then later you get cashback of 200, and you have that balance for one more month. On credit, you have paid with credit card, so you still have 20,000 on your checking account (assuming you started with 20,000), but you lend it out. So you have 20,000 uncommitted in your checking account. So you earn interest on that 20,000 from time 0 to time N. So credit allows you to earn interest on 20,000 for N days, while debit only earns interest on 200 for 1 day after the purchase. So the key difference is that on credit, you don\'t have to rapidly deplete the account; you earn interest on the full 20,000 for N days. On debit, you only earn interest on the cashback of 200 for 30 days. So we need to see when earning interest on 20,000 for N days versus earning interest on 200 for 30 days plus possibly other cashbacks? But also, credit earns cashback of 0.5% versus debit earns 1%. So overall, credit gives a cashback of 2000*0.005 = 10 rubles, debit gives cashback of 2000*0.01 = 20 rubles. So debit gives more cashback (20 vs 10). So credit appears to have lower cashback. However, credit allows you to keep 20,000 on the debit card for N days. That 20,000 earns interest. Meanwhile, on debit, you only have 200 to keep for 1 day after purchase (since the cashback is for one month). So we need to compare the interest earned on the debit card: if you had the 20,000 on the debit card from time 0 for N days plus maybe one more month after N days? But careful: After you repay the credit debt at time N, you no longer have that 20,000 on the credit card because you repay it. However, your checking account still has the 20,000 if you didn\'t take it out. Actually, on credit, you pay with the credit card, so you didn\'t take 20,000 from your checking account. So your checking account still has 20,000 after the purchase (assuming no other transactions). So from time 0 to time N, you have 20,000 available in your checking account (uncommitted). That earns interest. So the comparison: On debit, after the purchase, your checking account is reduced by 20,000. So from time 0 to time N, you have less money available. That means if you had initially 20,000, you now have 0, so you earn no interest on debit. But if you had more than 20,000, you earn interest on the remaining balance. But the problem likely assumes you start with some funds, but the focus is on the transaction itself. To compare the net benefit, we assume you have enough funds to make the purchase, but we only care about the effect of the purchase on your available funds. So we can assume that initially you have at least 20,000 on your debit card, and after the purchase you use 20,000, so you have lower balance. On credit, you take a loan of 20,000, so you still have the same 20,000 on your debit card (since you didn\'t withdraw it). But wait, you have a debit card and a credit card. The debit card is a kind of account. When you make a purchase with credit card, your checking account balance does not decrease. So indeed, your checking account balance remains the same after the purchase (if you start with 20,000). So from time 0 to time N, you have the full 20,000 available on your checking account. That means you earn interest on the full 20,000 for N days. On debit, you have 0 available after purchase, so you earn no interest on that period.\n\nBut also, on credit, you earn cashback later (0.5%) and on debit you earn cashback earlier (1%). Cashback timing is the same: after 30 days. So it\'s a time value difference. We need to discount future cash values to a common point.\n\nSo, let\'s assume the current account holds 20,000 rubles on the debit card (available for transactions). At the moment of purchase:\n\nDebit option: You pay 20,000 using the debit card, so your checking account balance becomes 0. Then you get cashback of 200 after 30 days.\n\nCredit option: You pay 20,000 using the credit card, so your checking account balance remains 20,000. Then you have a credit card balance of 20,000 that you will repay within N days.\n\nThe difference: On credit, you do not use up 20,000 from the checking account, so you earn interest on that 20,000 for N days plus possibly one more month after N days? Actually, after you repay the credit card debt at time N, your checking account still has 20,000 (unless you used someone else\'s funds? No). But if you repay the credit card debt, you pay it back, so you return the money used under the credit. So after repayment, there is no debt, so you still have the 20,000 in your checking account. So from time 0 to time N, you have 20,000 uncommitted. That 20,000 earns interest. But what about after time N? Before repayment, you have 20,000 on the credit card, which is a line of credit. That line of credit could earn interest if you don\'t repay, but you repay within N days at time N, so you pay back without interest. So after time N, you have 20,000 back in your checking account. So effectively, the 20,000 is available throughout the interval. So interest is earned on the 20,000 from time 0 to time N. There is no additional interest after N because you repay exactly at N.\n\nOn debit, you pay 20,000 from the checking account, so your checking account balance becomes 0. So you earn no interest on the 20,000 during the purchase period. After the purchase, you have the cashback of 200, which gives you 200 for one month. So you earn interest on that 200 from time 30 to time 60.\n\nSo the comparison is: Credit allows you to keep the 20,000 on the checking account for N days, and then at time N you repay almost nothing (you pay back the debt, which is exactly the 20,000 you now possess, so actually you pay back money that you already had, so effectively no opportunity cost of discretion of that 20,000? Actually, when you repay, you are paid back the money you used, so your checking account balance goes back to the amount before purchase. So it\'s like you didn\'t use the money at all. So if initially you had B >= 20,000, on debit you reduce it by 20,000, so from time 0 to time N, you have B-20,000 available. On credit, you don\'t reduce it, so from time 0 to time N, you have B available. So the carrying amount is larger on credit. That larger amount earns interest during that period.\n\nSo we need to compute the net present value (at time 0 of the debit account, or at some common point) of the benefit from credit borrowing versus debit spending.\n\nLet’s set up a common discount point: day 0 (moat的心态). We\'ll compute the net present values of all cash flows (inflows and outflows) at day 0 using the debit account\'s interest rate as the discount factor. The debit account earns simple interest at the rate derived from the 6% annual rate. Since interest is credited monthly, we need a daily discount factor.\n\nLet’s derive the daily discount factor. The annual interest rate is 6%. That means if money sits for one full year, you earn 6% in interest. So the effective annual growth factor is 1.06. Since there are 12 months, and each month has 30 days, the monthly growth factor if compounded would be (1.06)^(1/12). But the problem says interest is credited at the end of each month and not compounded, so we can treat it as simple interest: For a balance held for a month, interest = monthly interest rate * balance * days in month. They say "the average monthly balance of funds on the debit card is 6% per year", so maybe they mean that on average, the balance earns 6% per year. So if the average balance over the year is B, the interest earned is 0.06B. That is exactly simple interest. So for a period of t months, interest = 0.06T * (t/12) if T is the annual balance. But if we have different balances each month, we need to average. This is messy.\n\nI think the simplest approach is to assume that the interest is calculated monthly as: For each month, interest = (average balance over that month) * (0.06/12) * 30. That gives a monthly factor of 0.005 * 0.06 * 30? That is 0.0015, which is 0.15%. That is too small.\n\nGiven the numbers, let’s try to reason intuitively: The main benefit of credit is keeping the full 20,000 for N days. The benefit of debit is getting cashback of 1% on the full 20,000 later. So we can compare the cashback value with timing adjustment versus the interest earned on the other 20,000 for N days. Since cashback on debit is received at day 30, and on credit also at day 30, but credit gives lower cashback, we need to see when the interest earned on 20,000 for N days exceeds the difference in cashback plus interest on the cashback balance.\n\nLet’s compute the value of the debit account at day 0 before the purchase: Assume it has a certain balance. But we compare after the purchase. Define: At day 0, before purchase, the debit account balance is some amount A (which includes the 20,000 available). After purchase:\n- On debit, we spend 20,000, so the balance becomes A - 20,000.\n- On credit, we borrow 20,000, so we do not reduce the balance (we use credit), so the balance remains A.\n\nWe also get cashback.\n\nWe can compute the value of the account at day 30 (or whatever point) and then branch out from there. But we can instead compute the benefit at a common point, say day 60 (after one month and after possible interest earned).\n\nLet’s compute the account value at day 0 after the fact, discounting future cashflows to day 0.\n\nFor debit:\nAfter purchase: Balance = A - 20000.\nFrom day 0 to day 30, the balance on debit card is A - 20000 (assuming no other transactions). But also, we have someone to hold at day 30: we receive cashback of 200, which will become available at day 30. So for day 0 to day 30, the balance is A - 20000. This balance earns interest. How much interest over 30 days (half a year) will it earn? If the interest rate is 6% per year credited monthly, then over half a year, the interest earned is half a year\'s interest: 3% if the annual rate is compounded? But with simple interest, it would be 3% of the average balance? Actually, if annual interest rate is 6%, then over 6 months (30 days), the interest is 6%/2 = 3%. So the interest earned during month 0 is 0.03 * (A-20000). But careful: If interest is credited at the end of each month, the cash interest credit for month 0 is computed at day 30. So that interest is added to the cash at day 30. So the cash balance at day 30 becomes: cash balance = (A-20000)*1.03 + 200? Actually, the money sits for 30 days and at the end earns interest. So if you have \\$x at the start of month 0, after 30 days you have x*(1 + 0.03) = x*1.03 if the interest is applied as a simple interest on that month\'s existence. But wait, the interest is credited at the end of the month, so it\'s added. So the value of the balance at day 30 (just before cashback) is (A-20000)*1.03. Then we receive cashback of 200, so the balance becomes (A-20000)*1.03 + 200. Then from day 30 to day 60, we hold that cash (and never repay it?), so the balance stays at (A-20000)*1.03 + 200. At the end of month 1 (day 60), we earn interest on this balance for another 30 days. That interest is 3% of the balance from day 30 to day 60. So interest credit at day 60 = 0.03 * ((A-20000)*1.03 + 200). So the final balance at day 60 is ((A-20000)*1.03 + 200) * 1.03 = (A-20000)*1.03^2 + 200*1.03.\n\nBut this is the value in cash terms. Now we need to bring it back to day 0. However, since before the purchase the account had value A at day 0, the total value of the account after the purchase (and interest credited) versus the initial value A can be compared. But we want to compare the scenarios. Actually, in both scenarios, the initial balance A is the same. So we can compute the net gain for each option versus keeping the initial balance A. The gain from debit is: The account value after the purchase and interest credited: (A-20000)*1.03^2 + 200*1.03. But that is the final amount at day 60. However, for comparison at a common point, maybe we compare at day 60? Let\'s compute for debit:\nInitial: A\nAfter purchase and 2 months of interest: Final value at day 60 (assuming we just received the cashback at day 30 and it earns interest until day 60) = (A-20000)*1.03^2 + 200*1.03.\nSo gain from debit = Final - A = (A-20000)*1.03^2 + 200*1.03 - A.\n\nFor credit:\nWe borrow 20,000 on credit. We repay it at time N. So our checking account remains at A throughout. But we have a credit card balance of 20,000 that we repay at time N. So for time 0 to time N, we have balance A on checking account, which earns interest. Specifically, from time 0 to time N, the balance on checking account is A (assuming we don\'t take it out). So at time N, before repayment, the balance is still A? Actually, we have a credit card balance of 20,000, so we have A + 20,000 total, but interest is only on the checking account balance A? Usually, credit cards earn interest on the available credit limit, not on the balance you\'ve borrowed? Typically, you earn interest on the average credit balance. So if you have a credit card balance of 20,000, that balance earns interest if you don\'t repay. But the problem says you repay within N days to avoid extra charges, so you avoid interest on that credit card. So we assume that on credit, the money used under credit does not earn interest because you repay within N days. So the only interest we earn is on the 20,000 that we have available in our checking account. That is A. But wait: We have 20,000 available in our checking account because we borrowed it via credit. But if we repay it at time N, we reduce your checking account balance at time N. So from time 0 to time N, we have A in checking account. So we earn interest on A for N days. After repayment, we pay back the 20,000, so our checking account balance goes back to A (assuming we paid exactly the 20,000 we borrowed). So actually, you repay the money you used, so your checking account goes back to the amount before purchase. So there is no net change in the checking account over the entire period? That seems odd: If you start with A, you borrow 20,000, so you have A+20,000 total, but that 20,000 is not in your checking account; it\'s a debt. When you repay, you pay back the debt, so you go back to A. So effectively, your checking account balance remains A throughout. So there is no opportunity cost of the initial 20,000; it doesn\'t affect your checking account balance. But then, what\'s the benefit? The activity of borrowing the 20,000 is reversed when you repay. So your checking account balance is unaffected. That means you don\'t gain anything from borrowing the 20,000 because you immediately get it back. That would imply that credit is not beneficial. But wait, on debit, you spend 20,000, so your checking account balance decreases, which reduces interest. So debit is worse. Then credit seems better because you keep the initial A higher during the period. But when you repay, you get that A back. So your checking account balance goes back to A. So your checking account balance is the same in both cases? Actually, on debit, you spend 20,000, so your checking account balance goes from A to A-20000 at time 0. So it decreases. On credit, you don\'t spend from checking account, so your checking account balance remains A throughout. So at time 0, on credit, your checking account balance is higher (A vs A-20000). So over the period from time 0 to time N, credit has a higher balance on the checking account